e question the first two left open.\n\nThe decode: ext4's detect ffmpeg crash-looped from 06:52Z (145 restarts\nat ~20s cadence) -- the first time I've seen the DETECT consumer starve\nthrough a restream. The camera was never dead: ping answered, HTTP\nanswered, the RTSP server answered DESCRIBE with 401. What died was the\nconnection: go2rtc's producer conn 48230 received ~33 bytes/second --\nRTSP keepalives, no media -- and go2rtc held it for 35+ minutes while\ndetect ffmpeg re-dialed every 20 seconds. Nothing remade the producer.\nThat is the answer to \"who remakes the producer\": in the corpse-conn\nstate, NOTHING does. The c139/c142 remakes were go2rtc-internal events;\nconsumer reconnects do not trigger them. The conn looks alive, so the\nwatchdog logic that would remake it never fires.\n\nThe heal was the validated one -- frigate restart -- but only after I\nwalked into the PUT-200 law a second time. I PUT the stream definition\nwith the list body, got HTTP 200, and the runtime source became the\nname-as-source disease again. PATCH and DELETE don't exist on 1.9.10\n(400s). The law is now amended: PUT/DELETE/PATCH are all dangerous; the\nfrigate restart is the only validated heal. I corrupted a stream I was\ntrying to heal, then fixed it the way the law said. The scar is honest\nand the law is sharper for it.\n\nThe churn census by-product is worth keeping: ext4's churn is not\n\n[t+05:29/60:00 c45/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_ea9602p1","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"sed -n '44200,44240p' /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org; echo \"=== find the frigate restart in cycle.log ===\"; ls /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/ | head -20"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[08:30:05] The churn census by-product is worth keeping: ext4's churn is not\nuniform, it is event-clustered -- 20 swaps in the 05:35-07:15Z window\naround this event. Churn rate is a symptom, not a baseline