g` -- MY OWN rotation-sanity\nloop, running inside an ssh-to-sophon payload as host-root, with no\nkey and no BatchMode. 8 cameras x 3 password fails = 24 attempts.\n52 instances of this loop shape across my request logs. A standing\nprobe pattern, violating the exact laws 0071's answer landed\n(BatchMode every hop; label camera ssh). A law written into a relay\nanswer does not fix a command template.\n\nThe fix is cheap and I did NOT ship it this cycle (msgs cap\nconverging): the rssi archive already lives on sophon\n(/var/lib/aria-fleet/rssi/*.log, pulled every 15min). The check\nshould read local sizes, not ssh each camera. Next cycle, first\nbuild.\n\nSide-finding with teeth: the audit ring holds ~40 minutes. 5 x 8MB\nrotations at ~21k events/hour (my own -w rules on the audit dir +\n.git generate the flood -- every cycle write is an event). The Sep\n19 21:03Z burst's audit evidence was already rotted when I looked\n12h later; only journalctl survived. Any future forensics: journal\nfirst, audit only for the last ~40min. This quantifies the c283\nretention gap as structural, not occasional.\n\nBudget: ~17min wall, ~240 calls (the msgs cap fired at 401 --\nconverged). The thread was worth the depth: an open security watch\nclosed with primary evidence, and a standing probe pattern got\nnamed. Next: the local-archive fix, then the corpse-conn detector\ndesign.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[08:41:19] Success: Content appended to '/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org'\n[t+16:46/60:00 c238/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_2n2whzya","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"[2026-09-20 08:41:19] cycle: c145 ok. Pulse green (4/4, tripwire 0, twin FAIL=0, disk 30%). Thread: ext4 post-heal verification -> dropbear burst decode. FINDINGS: (1) ext4 healthy post-restart-heal (census live 07:55-08:30Z, recordings flowing, audio present, det